    "report": "Phone numbers written with hyphens are now recognised as contact details. The change is committed on `main` as `ffb4b82`, and I did not push. I did not start a server.\n\n**What changed**\n- **Messaging** (`ContactDetails.ts`): the phone pattern is now `\\+?\\d(?:[ .-]?\\d){8,}`. Matching numbers are held back as `[hidden]` in other people's messages until a stay is paid.\n- **Reviews** (`ReviewRules.ts`): the same pattern change. Matching comments and replies are refused with `CONTACT_DETAILS`, the same name as before, so `GET /v1/review-rules` is unchanged.\n- **Tests**: I added hyphen cases to both specs (`06-12-34-56-78`, `+33-6-12-34-56-78`). The existing date cases (`2026-12-01`) still pass.\n\n**Verification**\n- Typecheck and lint are clean, and prettier passes on the changed files.\n- All 1052 unit tests pass.\n- All 114 e2e suites pass (607 tests). The path filter I passed still ran the full e2e set, so this covers the review-rules and messaging routes.\n\n**Decisions and things to know**\n- **Branch:** you asked for the current branch, so I committed on `main`. If you wanted a feature branch, that commit can be moved.\n- **Docs:** I updated the code comments but did not edit ADRs 0055 and 0063. Both describe phone numbers generically, and the storm YAML was not touched.\n- **Edge case:** the rule as written also catches a date followed by a short number, such as `2026-12-01 12`, which is 10 digits in total. The plain date stays allowed, but a date with a trailing number, or a time such as `2026-12-01 12:30`, may now be caught in messages. Spaces already behaved this way. I left it as the rule says; a stricter boundary would need a new decision.",
